seg-143726cc3d5f = What is verified

# text
seg-029a9bb7608f = The chain of trust, from the root down to the data:

# text
seg-886f1b6556ce = <b>Public key (ICD { "3.2.2" }, { "6.2" }).</b> A DSM-PKR is reassembled from its blocks and its Merkle path is walked to a trusted Merkle root (SHA-256).

# text
seg-27b4935fe47b = <b>KROOT (ICD { "3.2.3" }, { "6.3" }).</b> A DSM-KROOT is reassembled; its ECDSA signature (P-256/SHA-256 or P-521/SHA-512) is checked against the public key it names, over the NMA header and the chain parameters.

# text
seg-acc1f06cf63a = <b>TESLA keys (ICD { "5.5" }, { "6.4" }).</b> Each key in a MACK message is walked back to the KROOT or to an earlier verified key, with the chain's hash function.

# text
seg-274285216a96 = <b>MACSEQ (ICD { "4.1.2" }, { "6.6" }).</b> The flexible Tag-Info fields are checked against the MACSEQ with the key that opens that MACK.

# text
seg-41ba810d7c8d = <b>Tags (ICD { "4.2" }, { "6.7" }).</b> Each tag is recomputed (HMAC-SHA-256 or CMAC-AES, truncated to the chain's tag length) over the satellite identities, the sub-frame time, the tag position, the NMA status and the navigation data it covers, and compared. ADKD { "0" } (ephemeris, clock and status), ADKD { "4" } (timing parameters) and ADKD { "12" } (slow MAC, key published ten sub-frames later) are handled, with cross-authentication of satellites that do not themselves carry OSNMA, and dummy tags (COP { "0" }).

# text
# c1: discarded
seg-1be16a4ca722 = A tag that names a reserved value, or whose ADKD does not match the MAC look-up table slot, is set aside (the Receiver Guidelines say to discard it) and reported as <c1/>, not as a failure.

# text
# c1: bad_crc
seg-eb993b3a0d17 = Pages are checked against their CRC (the Galileo CRC-24Q) before anything else; a page that fails is dropped and reported (<c1/>).

# text
seg-c414a30d0840 = Status values

# text
# c1: authenticated
# c2: failed
# c3: tag_mismatch
# c4: macseq_mismatch
# c5: key_chain_mismatch
# c6: key_conflict
# c7: pending
# c8: no_chain
# c9: awaiting_key
# c10: no_nav_data
# c11: unknown_maclt
# c12: unsupported_function
# c13: service_not_usable
# c14: chain_mismatch
# c15: discarded
# c16: authenticated
seg-268f61b565b9 = Per tag: <c1/>, <c2/> with a reason (<c3/>, <c4/>, <c5/>, <c6/>), <c7/> with a reason (<c8/>, <c9/>, <c10/>, <c11/>, <c12/>, <c13/>, <c14/>), or <c15/>. A dummy tag (COP { "0" }) shows as <c16/> at tag level, since it proves the key, but it covers no navigation data and never counts for a satellite.

# text
seg-331a6ab821f3 = Per satellite, in the form the receiver-trust monitor consumes:

# text
# c1: Authenticated
seg-9912068c5b34 = <c1/> only while the newest verified ephemeris and clock data (ADKD { "0" } or

# text
# c1: Unavailable
seg-38e7f56293e7 = of that satellite is recent (within { "600" } s of the verifier's time by default) and is still the data in use. The check is on the data, not the IODnav: the verifier keeps the SHA-256 of the authenticated bit string (Word Types { "1" } to { "5" } as ADKD { "0" } covers them) and of the newest such string the satellite sends, and they must be equal. A copied IODnav over other words, or altered Word Type { "5" } bits (which carry no IODnav), reads <c1/> until the new data authenticates. A sub-frame that carries ephemeris words but no complete set of Word Types { "1" } to { "5" } under one IODnav takes the status away in the same way (every genuine E1-B sub-frame carries all five); one with no ephemeris words at all says nothing. Tags of another satellite (cross authentication) count for the satellite whose data they cover.

# text
# c1: Failed
seg-233109ef4428 = <c1/> when a check on the satellite's data failed within the failure memory ({ "600" } s by default of the verifier's time), even if later data verifies. A mismatch is charged to the satellite whose data the tag covers and to the satellite that transmitted the tag, since either could be at fault; a MACSEQ mismatch is charged to the transmitting satellite.

# text
# c1: Unavailable
seg-8ba1d89bcf91 = <c1/> otherwise, and for every satellite after a verified alert message, with NMA status "don't use" or a reserved status, and until the first key has verified.

# text
# c1: Failed
# c2: Authenticated
# c3: Unavailable
# c4: $PKSOS,<A|F|N>[,<sat>:<A|F|N>...]
# c5: kshana::osnma::pksos_sentence
seg-7fceea6e23f8 = The overall value is <c1/> if any satellite failed, <c2/> if at least one is authenticated and none failed, otherwise <c3/>. <c4/> is the matching sentence; <c5/> builds it.

# text
seg-446a3218ccbb = Time

# text
# c1: set_reference_time
# c2: --reference-time
seg-18e91e6aa160 = The key chain shows that a key belongs to a given sub-frame, not that the sub-frame is the present one. The verifier therefore never takes "now" from an unverified page. Its time is the later of the reference time given with <c1/> (<c2/>) and the sub-frame of the latest TESLA key that verified (a key cannot be known before its slot, so it proves the time is no earlier). Failure memory, the validity window of an authentication and the eviction of old data run on that time. A sub-frame stamped more than three sub-frames past the latest verified key is set aside unless its own key proves its time; key and alert messages in it are still processed, since they carry their own proof (a signature or the Merkle root).

# text
# c1: set_reference_time
# c2: poll_clock
# c3: KeyStall
seg-318323322a3a = The clock stands still when OSNMA stops, since only keys move it. A live caller must call <c1/> with host time every sub-frame; authentication then expires on its own, and <c2/> (also run with every sub-frame) returns a <c3/> event once no key has verified for longer than the authentication window.

# text
# c1: freshness: NOT enforced
seg-5338d8f5de77 = What this does not give is freshness. Without a reference time from a source an attacker cannot move, a replay of old authentic data under old stamps authenticates and the page stamps are only as good as their source: the CLI says so (<c1/>) whenever no reference time is given. With a reference time the tolerance is below one sub-frame (default { "29" } s; larger values are refused). A reference time is a single instant: sub-frames further from it than the tolerance are set aside and counted, so it suits live or just-recorded data, not a long recording. For a recording, leave the reference time out and read the result as "authentic, not necessarily fresh". The Receiver Guidelines give the formal requirement.

# text
seg-aa37ce329914 = The page stamps of a u-blox stream come from the unauthenticated data itself (see Input), so they provide no freshness protection either.

# text
seg-a33d72076025 = Chains, keys and alerts

# text
# c1: chain_mismatch
seg-be2d9588df65 = Verified chains are kept by chain id (CID); tags are checked with the chain the NMA header names, and a header naming a chain that was not verified leaves the sub-frame's tags unchecked (<c1/>). A second KROOT of a chain in force adds an anchor; a KROOT of another chain is kept alongside, so announcing the old chain again after a renewal does not undo the new one.

# text
# c1: stale
seg-bbb6574fa258 = The NMA header is not authenticated, so what it announces counts only when confirmed: when more than one satellite sends the same header in a sub-frame, or when a verified KROOT's signed message carries it (the header is part of that message). The two are not equal. Agreement of more than one satellite protects against a single-satellite anomaly, not against a spoofer: one spoofer can fake two satellite ids. What that buys it is denial of service, and nothing more since a confirmed "don't use" clears every satellite's authenticated data: lifting it, forged or genuine, restores nothing until new tags verify under genuine operational sub-frames. (Before that clearing, a forged lift could bring back, for up to the authentication window, data that had already authenticated.) The real confirmation is the KROOT-signed header, which also lifts a "don't use" again when it carries an operational status. A single satellite's header changes nothing but its own sub-frame's tag checking. A KROOT whose time of applicability is not later than that of the chain held for the same id (a replay of an older one) is refused (<c1/>); a KROOT of the same chain adds an anchor.

# text
seg-5c08dbaa8d43 = The confirmed header's status and CPKS value drive revocation. With NMA status "don't use", CPKS "chain revoked" drops the chain the header names and refuses its KROOT from then on; CPKS "public key revoked" drops the public key that signed that chain, and every chain signed with it. If the replacement chain for that id was adopted before the header was confirmed, however long before, the key revoked is the previous chain's, not the new one's; that memory is kept until the revocation is carried out or another procedure's header is confirmed, and a revocation header repeated every sub-frame is carried out once. The same CPKS values with status operational mark the transition to the replacement and revoke nothing. The header is not itself authenticated, so a forged revocation can only take authentication away, and it is final until the verifier is restarted. That finality covers the live key too: a forged public key revocation resets the memory of the superseded key and revokes the key in use, and only a restart undoes it.

# text
# c1: Unavailable
seg-e7d43eb18c24 = A DSM-PKR with NPKT { "4" } is the alert message; it is verified through the Merkle tree like a key, its random NPK field being part of the leaf. A verified alert clears all chains and public keys, refuses every later KROOT and public key, and makes every satellite <c1/>, as the Receiver Guidelines direct, until the verifier is restarted.

# text
seg-4068e9bda7c1 = A DSM-KROOT's padding field is a hash of the signed message and the signature. It is checked before the signature, and it also says which NMA header the signed message was built with when the header changed while the message was being assembled.

# text
seg-8ea31bf25ef0 = What is not verified, or not exercised

# text
seg-3d0808cab632 = <b>Freshness.</b> See Time above: it needs a reference time from outside.

# text
seg-b3d63950baa4 = <b>Renewal and revocation.</b> Chains and keys are followed as the streams of the published vectors exercise them (renewal, end of chain, chain and key revocation, new public key, new Merkle tree, alert), not every case the ICD allows. A revocation announced by the unauthenticated header is taken at its word and is final for the session. A new Merkle tree is picked up only through the Merkle root the verifier is given.

# text
seg-f01d90a44585 = <b>Merkle tree hash.</b> DSM-PKR messages are checked with a SHA-256 tree. A SHA3-256 tree, which the ICD allows in future, is not supported. The chain hash function can be SHA-256 or SHA3-256.

# text
seg-d9ca4e69ffc9 = <b>Key status lists.</b> Revocation is followed through the NMA header and the messages in the stream; certificate revocation lists are not read.

# text
seg-73e0b16d03c7 = <b>Authenticated data coverage.</b> ADKD { "4" } with COP { "1" } sent in an odd sub-frame cannot authenticate (Word Type { "10" } may be sent one or two sub-frames earlier); this costs availability only, never correctness.

# text
seg-acc28dcced83 = <b>Constant-time operation.</b> Not provided and not needed: the verifier holds no secrets (public keys, published chain keys and received data only).

# text
seg-b2f8ec90ae39 = <b>Signal and data quality.</b> Pages are CRC-checked and alert pages dropped, but nothing is known of the signal they arrived on. Reed-Solomon recovery of I/NAV words { "1" } to { "4" } is not used: data must be received directly.

# text
seg-97313ba2bf88 = <b>Not a position integrity service.</b> Authentication says the data came from the system. It says nothing about the signal it arrived on or the quality of the fix.

# text
seg-94058315dfe4 = Input

# text
# c1: <svid> <gst_seconds> <60 hex digits>
# c2: #
# c3: gst_seconds
# c4: week * 604800 + time of week
seg-d696f298c4a2 = <b>Plain page format.</b> One page pair per line: <c1/>, <c2/> comments allowed. <c3/> is GST in seconds (<c4/>) at the start of the page; the hex is the { "240" } bits of the even and odd halves (tails included), most significant bit first.

# text
# c1: SVID,NumNavBits,NavBitsHEX
# c2: --start-gst
seg-e436d96b12cf = <b>Test-vector CSV.</b> The layout of the published vectors (<c1/>). The start time comes from <c2/> or from a dated file name.

# text
# c1: --format ubx
# c2: src/osnma/ubx.rs
# c3: src/realdata/ubx.rs
seg-72132e3796bc = <b>u-blox UBX-RXM-SFRBX.</b> A raw UBX byte stream (<c1/>, detected automatically). Galileo E1-B I/NAV messages are rebuilt into page pairs (<c2/>); other systems, E5b, alert pages and frames with a bad checksum are ignored. SFRBX carries no time, so each page is stamped from the time inside its own data (word types { "5" } and { "0" }) and accepted only if consecutive time-bearing pages agree with the number of pages received between them; pages that cannot be placed are dropped, never guessed. That time comes from the data itself and gives no freshness protection (see Time above). Signal id { "0" } (E1-C) carries no data, so accepting it alongside E1-B is harmless. A stray sync pair with an absurd length does not hold the reader back. The framing code is a third copy of the UBX framing in the tree (<c3/>, the receiver-trust ingest); it is kept apart so that receiver-trust behaviour is unchanged, and the three could share one reader later. The word layout of an SFRBX message is taken from public descriptions and checked on synthetic frames only, not on a live receiver.

# text
seg-fcd0ebea49bc = Command line

# text
# c1: --epochs
# c2: --json
seg-47118a577876 = Text output gives the status per satellite and, with <c1/>, per tag and epoch; <c2/> gives the same with the tag list. Exit status { "3" } means a check on some satellite's data failed, { "2" } means a usage or input error. The advisory above is printed first on every path, errors and usage included.

